# UID LED on/off

The beacon LED (blue UID LED) helps identify a switch in a crowded closet. These EEM applets take an optional stack member number and default to switch 1, so the same configuration works on standalone switches and stacks.

If the applets already exist, remove them first. Re-entering an applet with the same name adds to the existing actions instead of replacing them:

```text
no event manager applet BEACON-ON
no event manager applet BEACON-OFF
```

```text
event manager applet BEACON-ON
 event none
 action 1.0 if $_none_argc eq "0"
 action 1.1  set sw "1"
 action 1.2 else
 action 1.3  set sw "$_none_arg1"
 action 1.4 end
 action 2.0 cli command "enable"
 action 2.1 cli command "conf t"
 action 2.2 cli command "hw-module beacon on switch $sw"
!
event manager applet BEACON-OFF
 event none
 action 1.0 if $_none_argc eq "0"
 action 1.1  set sw "1"
 action 1.2 else
 action 1.3  set sw "$_none_arg1"
 action 1.4 end
 action 2.0 cli command "enable"
 action 2.1 cli command "conf t"
 action 2.2 cli command "hw-module beacon off switch $sw"
!
alias exec bon event manager run BEACON-ON
alias exec boff event manager run BEACON-OFF
```

Usage:

- `bon` / `boff` - turn the beacon on or off on switch 1 (standalone switch)
- `bon 3` / `boff 3` - turn the beacon on or off on stack member 3

`$_none_argc` is the number of arguments passed to `event manager run`, and `$_none_arg1` is the first argument. The alias passes anything typed after `bon` through to the applet. Stack member numbers come from `show switch`.

**Note:** Tested on standalone switches only. Not yet tested on a stack. If the `if` block causes problems on a stack, remove actions 1.0-1.4 and change `$sw` to `$_none_arg1`. The switch number is then required every time, including `bon 1`.
